{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011Ce4mWAxNjEEGxwNxfk26m","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Placement Decision\n\n**Where to add it:**\n\nIn **§2, Failure handling**, fold it into the second bullet (Timeouts are explicit) as follows:\n\n```markdown\n## 2. Failure handling\n\n- **Retries are bounded and jittered.** Every retry wrapper declares\n  its maximum attempt count at the call site; backoff is exponential\n  with jitter, and a non-retriable error class aborts immediately —\n  enumerate those classes beside the wrapper, not in a distant\n  constants file.\n- **Timeouts are explicit.** No call inherits a library default\n  timeout silently; the call site states its per-attempt timeout.\n  A retry wrapper must also declare a wall-clock cap on total elapsed\n  time at the call site, since a per-attempt timeout with unbounded\n  attempts can still hang the caller.\n```\n\n**Rationale:**\n\nThe new rule's core constraint—that *both* attempt cap and wall-clock cap must be declared—is already owned by §2's two bullets: attempt count by the first bullet (Retries are bounded), wall-clock time by the second (Timeouts are explicit). The new rule strengthens the second bullet by making explicit the interaction between the two (unbounded retries + per-attempt timeout = hang risk) and mandating their joint declaration. This is a natural clause addition to the host bullet, not a freestanding concern."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":645,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":319,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}}
